# Target C-II — corrected joint-feasibility O--O path/hub/strip extends through `t<=1.861`

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only corrected high-`t` structural strengthening  
**Scope:** full-core/no-macroscopic-container Type-II branch with `t=(q-k)/D<=1.861`  
**Dependencies:** corrected `t<=1.860` joint-feasibility theorem; hostile-audited shared retention/complement incidence; retained O--O pair-energy budget; Section-157 bracing; exact laminar multishear and diameter-path Helly hub  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_joint_feasible_opposite_branch_path_t1861.py`  
**Supersedes quantitatively:** `target_c_joint_feasible_opposite_branch_path_t1860_2026-09-15.md` at the endpoint only; all topology is inherited from the corrected proof  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The corrected `t<=1.860` theorem repaired the high-`t` path argument by coupling retained
outer mass and outer energy before extracting the low-energy O--O family.  Its choice
of terminal-energy threshold `lambda=1/2` made the outer-energy variable cancel
exactly, but that choice is not forced.

Above `1.860`, the crude outer-energy ceiling is slightly too large for the same-branch
exclusion at `lambda=1/2`.  Lower the threshold to

\[
\boxed{\lambda={997\over2000}=0.4985.}
\tag{J61.1}
\]

The same joint-feasibility argument, now evaluated at

\[
T={1861\over1000}=1.861,
\]

forces

\[
\boxed{z>2.100-o(1),}
\tag{J61.2}
\]

while the matched atomic floor gives

\[
\boxed{\delta<0.09+o(1).}
\tag{J61.3}
\]

The lower threshold restores a strict opposite-branch geometric margin despite the
larger `delta` ceiling.  Weighted Markov then gives retained low-energy dominant O--O
pair weight

\[
\boxed{P_{low}>0.0081-o(1),}
\tag{J61.4}
\]

so one exact dominant phase contains more than

\[
\boxed{0.0027D-o(D)}
\tag{J61.5}

actual O--O trains whose terminal energy is at most `0.4985`.

Every such train uses opposite oriented terminal branches.  Since `lambda<1/2`, its
two terminal arcs are in fact separated by slightly more than `pi/3`.  Therefore the
selected same-phase family has a simple complement-dual path, every actual diameter
support interval contains one common dual hub, and switching is one-sided at every
nonhub path boundary.

Finally, the global switch-count budget gives a nonhub adjacent boundary with at most

\[
\boxed{2327}
\tag{J61.6}

moving actual diameter rows.  Its exact nonzero strip reaction is generated by one or
two actual switching diameter bars, as in the parent theorem.

This pushes the corrected path/hub/one-sided-strip normal form from `1.860` to `1.861`
without reusing any of the retracted projective-branch shortcuts.

---

## 1. Retained-mass feasibility at `T=1.861`

Keep the parent notation

\[
U={9t\over2}-2\pi-2\delta-\kappa/2,
\qquad
\kappa={k\over D}\ge0,
\tag{J61.7}
\]

and

\[
C_{76}=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0076,
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3}.
\]

At `t<=T`,

\[
U_0={9T\over2}-2\pi
\tag{J61.8}
\]

is the adverse value before outer energy is fed back.  Since `U>=C76-o(1)`,

\[
\delta
\le {U_0-C_{76}\over2}+o(1).
\tag{J61.9}
\]

The exact certificate gives

\[
U_0<2.091315,
\qquad
\delta<0.089545+o(1)<0.09+o(1).
\tag{J61.10}
\]

Use exactly the corrected parent feasibility system.  If retained dominant outer mass
is `z`, then outer energy gives

\[
\delta\ge F(z)-o(1),
\qquad
F(z)=z-6\sin(z/6),
\tag{J61.11}
\]

and the shared-retention/complement-incidence inequalities reduce to

\[
G(\delta,z)\ge-o(1).
\tag{J61.12}
\]

As in the parent theorem, `G_delta<0` on the whole relevant rectangle, so the weakest
feasibility at fixed `z` occurs at `delta=F(z)`.  Put

\[
H(z):=G(F(z),z).
\]

The same derivative argument remains valid at the new endpoint.  The exact verifier
checks

\[
5U_0^2<32\Delta_2,
\]

uses `r<1.05`, `1-delta>0.91`, and obtains

\[
G_\delta>-7.5,
\qquad
G_z>{24\over25}
\tag{J61.13}
\]

for `0<=z<=2.100`.  Since

\[
F'(z)<{1\over16},
\]

it follows that

\[
H'(z)>0
\tag{J61.14}
\]

throughout that interval.

The outward rational/Taylor evaluation gives

\[
\boxed{H(2.100)<-0.00108.}
\tag{J61.15}
\]

Hence `z<=2.100-o(1)` violates the necessary feasibility condition, proving (J61.2).

---

## 2. General low-energy extraction below `lambda=1/2`

Let `P_OO^dom` be retained dominant O--O pair weight.  The parent pair theorem gives

\[
P_{OO}^{dom}\ge z-U-o(1).
\tag{J61.16}
\]

For one retained O--O train `tau`, let `w_tau` be its pair weight and `e_tau` its exact
two-terminal directional energy.  The audited budget is

\[
{1\over D}\sum_\tau w_\tau e_\tau\le\delta+o(1).
\tag{J61.17}
\]

Select

\[
e_\tau\le\lambda={997\over2000}.
\]

Weighted Markov gives

\[
P_{low}
\ge P_{OO}^{dom}-{\delta\over\lambda}-o(1).
\tag{J61.18}
\]

Using (J61.7),

\[
\begin{aligned}
P_{low}
&\ge z-U-{\delta\over\lambda}-o(1)\\
&=z-\left({9t\over2}-2\pi\right)
  +{\kappa\over2}
  +\delta\left(2-{1\over\lambda}\right)-o(1).
\end{aligned}
\tag{J61.19}
\]

Now `lambda<1/2`, so the coefficient of `delta` is negative.  Adversely set `t=T`,
`kappa=0`, and use the upper bound (J61.10).  With `z>2.100-o(1)`, the exact certificate
proves

\[
\boxed{P_{low}>0.008146-o(1)>0.0081-o(1).}
\tag{J61.20}
\]

There are at most three exact phase windows, so one phase carries retained pair weight
above

\[
0.002715-o(1)>0.0027-o(1).
\]

Since pair weight per complete train is at most one, this proves (J61.5).

---

## 3. The lower threshold restores opposite terminal branches

Take one selected train.  The audited endpoint-energy theorem gives deviations

\[
0\le\alpha,\beta\le{\pi\over2}
\]

with

\[
e_\tau
\ge(1-\cos\alpha)+(1-\cos\beta)-o(1).
\tag{J61.21}
\]

If both terminals used the same oriented normal branch, convexity gives the same-branch
chord upper bound

\[
{r_\tau^2\over D^2}
\le
\lambda-{\lambda^2\over4}+o(1).
\tag{J61.22}
\]

Section-157 bracing gives

\[
{r_\tau\over D}
\ge1-\delta-{e_\tau\over2}-o(1)
\ge1-0.09-{\lambda\over2}-o(1).
\tag{J61.23}
\]

The exact rational certificate checks

\[
\boxed{
\left(1-0.09-{\lambda\over2}\right)^2
-\left(\lambda-{\lambda^2\over4}\right)
=0.000216125>0.
}
\tag{J61.24}
\]

Equations (J61.22)--(J61.24) contradict same-branch orientation.  Therefore every
selected train uses opposite oriented terminal branches.

This is the corrected orientation mechanism.  It uses the physical Section-157 chord
lower bound and does not infer oriented branches from projective energy alone.

---

## 4. Fixed separated arcs, path, and common hub

Each endpoint contribution in (J61.21) is at most `lambda+o(1)`, so

\[
\cos\alpha,\cos\beta
\ge1-\lambda-o(1)
=0.5015-o(1).
\]

Hence

\[
\alpha,\beta<\arccos(0.5015)+o(1)<{\pi\over3}+o(1).
\tag{J61.25}
\]

Since the oriented ideal normals are antipodal, the two terminal host arcs have a
fixed positive gap, in fact slightly larger than `pi/3` in the limit.

The selected trains lie in one shrinking exact projective phase window and are
pairwise noncrossing.  Proper crosscuts connecting the same two disjoint boundary arcs
are totally ordered, so their complement dual is a simple path of length

\[
>0.0027D-o(D).
\tag{J61.26}
\]

The exact laminar multishear theorem applies.  The diameter-path Helly theorem gives one
common region `R_*` on every actual diameter support interval.  Consequently switching
at every nonhub path boundary is one-sided toward the hub, exactly as in the parent
`1.860` theorem.

---

## 5. A nonhub strip has at most `2327` moving diameter rows

Let `N_i` be the number of actual diameter intervals starting or stopping at an
internal selected path boundary.  Every actual diameter interval contributes to at
most two such boundaries, so

\[
\sum_iN_i\le2|H|\le2\pi D.
\tag{J61.27}
\]

Discard the single hub boundary.  The remaining number of internal boundaries is still

\[
>0.0027D-o(D).
\]

Using `pi<355/113`,

\[
{2(355/113)\over0.0027}
=2327.1058\ldots<2328.
\tag{J61.28}
\]

If every nonhub boundary had at least `2328` switching rows, its average would be at
least `2328`, contradicting (J61.28).  Therefore one nonhub adjacent boundary has
integer switch count

\[
\boxed{N_i\le2327.}
\]

All these moving diameter rows are one-sided toward the common hub.  The existing
reaction-nondegeneracy and conic-Caratheodory theorems then give an exact nonzero layer
reaction generated by one or two actual switching diameter vectors.

---

## 6. Frontier effect and numerical limit of this continuation

The corrected live normal form is now

```text
t > 1.844 globally;
if t <= 1.861:
    joint feasibility forces z > 2.100;
    threshold lambda=0.4985 leaves >0.0081 D low-energy O-O pair weight;
    one exact phase has >0.0027 D actual O-O trains;
    physical bracing forces opposite oriented terminal branches;
    exact complement-dual path and common diameter-routing hub;
    one-sided switching at every nonhub boundary;
    one nonhub strip has <=2327 moving diameter rows;
    exact nonzero strip reaction generated by <=2 actual bars.
```

A non-load-bearing floating-point optimization of this same two-parameter continuation
places the zero-low-mass endpoint near

\[
T\approx1.86180.
\]

Thus the present architecture has only about `8e-4` further scalar room before the
low-energy path mass disappears.  Further progress should either sharpen the retained-
mass feasibility input or exploit the finite one-sided strip, rather than chase the
continuous root without structural gain.

No exact unit triangle or strict-diameter competitor is claimed.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only extension of the corrected joint-feasibility
opposite-branch path/hub/strip theorem through `t<=1.861`.  C-II and Level I remain
open.
